#### Article 6

1. Every wife during the marriage bond has the same rights and responsibilities as her husband over all matters relating to their marital life, relationship with their children, and ownership and management of joint property in accordance with applicable laws and regulations.  
2. After the dissolution of marriage, a woman has the same rights and responsibilities as her former husband over all matters relating to children with due regard to the best interests of the child.  
3. After the dissolution of marriage, a woman has the same rights as her former husband over all matters relating to joint property without prejudice to the rights of the child, in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.

#### Article 7

1. Regional Government has the obligation and responsibility for the empowerment and protection of women.  
2. Efforts for empowerment and protection of women as referred to in paragraph (1) include: a. determination of policies, programs, and activities to improve the quality of life of women related to the fields of development, education, health, economy, law, Human Rights and politics, environment and socio-culture;  
b. provision of services to women especially to victims of violence, female workers, elderly women, and women with disabilities, victims of conflict and victims affected by disasters; and  
c. implementation of gender mainstreaming in government institutions, women's study centers, research and development institutions, non-governmental institutions.  
3. Efforts for empowerment and protection of women as referred to in paragraph (2) shall be carried out in an integrated manner in accordance with applicable laws and regulations.  
4. Empowerment and protection of women shall be coordinated by the Regional Apparatus that administers regional government affairs in the field of women's empowerment and child protection.

## Part Two

Women's Empowerment

#### Article 8

Women's empowerment is directed to obtain opportunities and rights as human beings to be able to play a role and participate in the fields of: a. economy;  
b. socio-culture;  
c. politics; and  
d. law.

#### Article 9

Women's empowerment in the economic field as referred to in Article 8 letter a shall be implemented through:  
a. provision of skills and job training;  
b. facilitation of the formation of productive economic business groups;  
c. facilitation of strengthening and development of productive economic business groups;  
d. facilitation and capital assistance; and  
e. facilitation of marketing network development.

#### Article 10

Women's empowerment in the socio-cultural field as referred to in Article 8 letter b shall be implemented through:  
a. improvement of knowledge, attitudes and skills to encourage the fulfillment of tiered education in accordance with potential to improve social status;  
b. improvement of awareness, willingness and ability to overcome health problems through quality promotive, preventive, curative, and rehabilitative efforts, especially in the field of reproductive health;  
c. improvement of awareness and knowledge about independent, healthy and prosperous family planning including access to consultation services and marriage registration; and  
d. facilitation and efforts to preserve customs and development of science, technology, arts and culture for the advancement of women.

#### Article 11

1. Women's empowerment in the political field as referred to in Article 8 letter c includes: a. involvement of women in decision-making at various levels;  
b. provision of opportunities for women to hold public office;  
c. participation in general elections; and  
d. self-development through organizations to associate, assemble and express opinions.  
2. Women's empowerment in the political field as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be implemented in accordance with applicable laws and regulations.

#### Article 12

1. Women's empowerment in the legal field as referred to in Article 8 letter d includes: a. improvement of awareness and knowledge in the legal field through communication, information and education services; and  
b. facilitation of access and legal consultation services.  
2. Women's empowerment in the legal field as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be implemented in accordance with applicable laws and regulations.

## Part Three

Protection of Women

#### Article 13

1. Protection of women includes: a. social protection;  
b. economic protection; and  
c. legal protection.  
2. Social protection as referred to in paragraph (1) letter a is to ensure the survival of women in the form of social protection to ensure the fulfillment of decent living needs.  
3. Economic protection as referred to in paragraph (1) letter b is to prevent and address the risk of vulnerability due to shocks and social vulnerability of a person, family, group and/or community for the survival of women.  
4. Legal protection as referred to in paragraph (1) letter c shall be implemented in terms of providing protection to women who become witnesses and victims of violence and discrimination.

# CHAPTER III

## CHILD PROTECTION

## Part One

General

#### Article 14

Every Child has basic rights which include: a. civil rights and freedoms;  
b. family environment and alternative care;  
c. basic health and welfare;  
d. education, leisure time and cultural activities; and  
e. special protection.

## Part Two

Civil Rights and Freedoms

#### Article 15

Every Child has the right to obtain Civil Rights and Freedoms as referred to in Article 14 letter a in the form of: a. birth certificate;  
b. child identity card;  
c. expressing opinions in accordance with their age and level of intelligence;  
d. obtaining healthy and safe information;  
e. freedom to assemble and organize that is appropriate for them;  
f. protection of reputation and not being exploited to the public without the permission of the child; and/or  
g. participating in development through children's forums at the village/sub-district, sub-district and even Regency levels.

#### Article 16

In fulfilling the Civil Rights and Freedoms of Children, the Family: a. arranges the child's birth certificate after the child is born;  
b. obtains a child identity card;  
c. provides space to assemble and organize and listens to the child to express their opinion;  
d. supervises the child in accessing various information and provides healthy and safe information; and  
e. protects the child's privacy and does not expose without the child's permission.

#### Article 17

The Government provides facilities for the fulfillment of Civil Rights and Freedoms of Children, by: a. providing fast and easy services in making birth certificates;  
b. involving children through children's forums at the village/sub-district, sub-district and city levels in development planning deliberations or other similar forums;  
c. providing a children's call center as a means of interactive communication or complaints related to the interests of children;  
d. providing healthy and safe information facilities by supervising internet service providers;  
e. providing child identity cards; and  
f. providing reading rooms or reading corners in public spaces.

## Part Three

Rights in Family Environment and Alternative Care

#### Article 18

Every Child has the right to obtain rights in the family environment and alternative care as referred to in Article 14 letter b in the form of: a. obtaining priority to be raised by their own parents;  
b. not being separated from their parents, except such separation is in the best interests of the child;  
c. obtaining good, polite and affectionate parenting;  
d. obtaining balanced parenting from both parents;  
e. obtaining welfare support even though their parents are unable;  
f. obtaining alternative care in the event that both parents die or suffer from an illness that makes it impossible to care for the child; and  
g. obtaining family harmony.

